/** \page compositingpage Cartesian conversions and composites


\tableofcontents

\b Rack can also create radar image composites, sometimes called mosaics. In weather services, composites are often preferred to single-radar products.
This section lists related commands and examples. A list of all the related command is \ref commands_cart.
Motion vectors are a separate topic, discussed in \ref motion-composites .

\section cartesian  Mapping data from polar to Cartesian coordinates

A single-radar Cartesian image is created simply with \c --cCreate (abbr. \c -c ) as follows:

\~conf{.sh}
{
  "shell": false,
  "workdir": "env/default",
  "append_args": [
      "--encoding C -o out/cartesian-default-C.png",
      "--encoding S -o out/cartesian-default-S.png"
  ],
  "tests": {
     "out/cartesian-default-C.png": true
  }
}
\~

\code{.sh}
rack volume.h5 --cCreate -o cartesian.h5
\endcode

\~conf{.sh}
{
}
\~

\image html  cart-single-radar.png  "A single-radar Cartesian image produced directly with --cCreate." 
\image latex cart-single-radar.png  "A single-radar Cartesian image produced directly with --cCreate." 

By default, the first data in the input volume – \c /dataset1/data1 – is used.
This can be changed with \c --select , which has several options:
\include{doc} select.hlp.md

The following command loops through for quantities and displays them as an image panel:

\~conf{.sh}
{
  "shell": true,
  "workdir": "env/composite-fi",
  "prepend_args": "--cSize 300",
  "tests": {
     "out/quantities-panel.svg": true,
     "out/quantities-panel.svg.png": true
  }
}
\~

\code{.sh}
rack --outputPrefix $PWD/out/ \
    --forEach i=DBZH,VRADH,ZDR,RHOHV \
        --script '--select quantity=${i} -c -P -o cartesian-${i}.png' \
    volume.h5 \
    -o quantities-panel.svg
\endcode

\image html  quantities-panel.svg.png  "A panel of radar quantities."
\image latex quantities-panel.svg.png  "A panel of radar quantities."


The following command generates Pseudo Cappi images on predefined altitudes :

\~conf{.sh}
{
  "shell": true,
  "workdir": "env/composite-fi",
  "prepend_args": "--cSize 300",
  "tests": {
     "out/cappi-panel.svg": true,
     "out/cappi-panel.svg.png": true
  }
}
\~

\code{.sh}
rack --outputPrefix $PWD/out/ \
    --forEach i=600,1000,1200,2000 \
        --script '--select prf=SINGLE,quantity=DBZH --pCappi ${i} -c -P -o cartesian-cappi-${i}.png' \
    volume.h5 \
    -o cappi-panel.svg
\endcode

\image html  cappi-panel.svg.png  "A panel of pseudo CAPPI at several altitudes."
\image latex cappi-panel.svg.png  "A panel of pseudo CAPPI at several altitudes."



\~stop
\~

For more details on using \c --forEach and \c --script commands, see \ref scriptspage .


Further, if a polar product has been computed, its (main) output quantity is automatically used
but can be likewise overridden with \c --select .

The default settings of Cartesian transformation are collected below as command line options.

| Option                 | Parameters                 | Default                     |
| ---------------------- | -------------------------- | --------------------------- | 
| \c --cProj             | EPSG code, proj string, or "AEQD" | \c "AEQD" (Azimuthal Equidistant centered at the radar) |
| \c --cBBox             | xLL,yLL,xUR,yUR            | Matched to the radar range  | 
| \c --cSize             | width,height               | \c "500,500"                | 
| \c --cMethod           | compositing method         | \c "MAXIMUM"                |
| \c --select            | path,quantity,...          | \c "path=dataset1/data1"    |

In \b Rack , any single-radar Cartesian image is technically a "single-radar composite". 


\section compositing  Compositing

Internally, a composite is implemented as a separate memory array that supports \e continuous \e accumulation of data. 
That means that arbitrarily many input volumes can be added. 

\~conf{.sh}
{
  "tests": false
}
\~

\code{.sh}
rack \
     --cProj '5120' \
     --cSize '1024,1280' \
     --cBBox '-1100000,350000,948000,2910000' \
     data/volume-fikor.h5 --select quantity=DBZH --cAdd \
     data/volume-fikan.h5 --select quantity=DBZH --cAdd \
     data/volume-fivih.h5 --select quantity=DBZH --cAdd \
     ...
        ...
	   ...
     --cExtract DATA,WEIGHT \
     -o out/composite-test.h5 
\endcode

The first \c --cAdd command initialises the composite with current geographical settings.
As they are not given explicitly in this example, the composite applies AEQD projection centered around the first input (from \c fikor , Korpo radar).

\note For clarity, the applied \c --select command is repeated in the above example. It would be sufficient to put it before the first \c --cAdd command only
– the selector is internally stored as a \e composite \e input \e selector for subsequent inputs.

Instead of repeating arguments on the command line, it is handy to use \c --script to define a routine repeated for each input. (More details on \ref scriptspage page.)

\~conf{.sh}
{
  "shell": true,
  "workdir": "env/composite-nordic",
  "append_args": [
     "-o 'out/composite-nordic.tif'",
     "-o 'out/composite-nordic.txt'",
     "--outputConf 'svg:paths=RELATIVE'",
     "--gStyle '.IMAGE_BORDER=stroke:gray;width:1px'",
     "--gLinkImage 'out/map-composite-nordic.png'",
     "--gAlign 'HORZ_FILL:VERT_FILL'",
     "--paletteDefault --imageTransp 'nodata=1,undetect=0' -o 'out/composite-nordic_DATA.png'",
     "-o 'out/composite-nordic.svg'"
     ],
  "tests": {
     "out/composite-nordic.h5":  true,
     "out/composite-nordic.tif": [
        {"similarity": 0.95},
        {"filename": "reference/composite-nordic-AEQD.tif", "similarity": 0.95, "on_fail": "warn"}
       ],
     "out/composite-nordic.txt": true,
     "out/composite-nordic_DATA.png": {"similarity": 0.95},
     "out/composite-nordic.svg.png": {"similarity": 0.95}
    }
}
\~

\code{.sh}
rack \
     --cProj '5120' \
     --cSize '1024,1280' \
     --cBBox '-1100000,350000,948000,2910000' \
     --script '--pCappi 600 --cAdd' \
     data/*.h5 \
     --cExtract DATA,WEIGHT \
     -o out/composite-nordic.h5 
\endcode


\image html  composite-nordic.svg.png  "Appending several radars in a Cartesian image."
\image latex composite-nordic.svg.png  "Appending several radars in a Cartesian image."

\~stop
\~


Likewise, if file paths are long, their shared parts can be set with \c --inputPrefix and \c --outputPrefix . 
The previous command can be hence reformulated as follows, with an extended set of output files:
\~conf{.sh}
{
    "shell": true,
    "tests": {
        "out/composite-test.h5":  true,
        "out/composite-test.tif": [
            {"similarity": 0.95},
            {"filename": "reference/composite-test-AEQD.tif", "similarity": 0.95, "on_fail": "warn"}
        ],
        "out/composite-test.png": {"similarity": 0.95},
        "out/composite-test.txt": true
    }
}
\~

\~remark
	"on_fail": "error|warn|ignore"
\~


\code{.sh}
rack \
    --script '--select quantity=DBZH --cAdd' \
    volume-fikor.h5 \
    volume-fikan.h5 \
    volume-fivih.h5 \
    --cExtract DATA,WEIGHT \
    --outputPrefix out/ \
    -o composite-simple.h5 \
    -o composite-simple.tif \
    --palette default -o composite-simple.png \
    -o composite-simple.txt
\endcode


\note Subcommand \c --cCreate producing a single radar Cartesian image
is actually a shorthand of <tt>--cAdd --cExtract 'DATA,WEIGHT'</tt> .


\~remark
stop
\~


\~remark 
rack \
    --cSize '640,768' \
    --cProj '3067' \
    --cBBox '-208000.0,6390000.0,1072000.0,7926000.0' \
    --script '--select quantity=DBZH --cAdd' \
    data/*.h5 \
    --cExtract DATA \
    -o composite-fi.tif

EPSG:3035 — LAEA Europe. Equal-area, official EEA/Eurostat standard
for European analysis. Reasonable distortion across all of
Finland. Best choice for areal statistics and environmental data.

EPSG:3034 — LCC Europe. Lambert Conformal Conic
(shape-preserving). Used for pan-European aeronautical
charting. Better than LAEA if angular fidelity matters more than area.

EPSG:4258 — ETRS89 geographic. Not a projected CRS (it's lat/lon), but
it is the European datum that all Finnish official projections are
built on. Good for data exchange.

Critical gap in the list: the actual Finnish national projection
EPSG:3067 (ETRS89 / TM35FIN, central meridian 27°E) is absent. That is
what Finnish authorities (NLS, FMI, SYKE) use and what rack presumably
targets. EPSG:25833 (UTM zone 33, central meridian 15°E) is 12° west
of Finland's center — it works but accumulates distortion. EPSG:3067
should probably be added explicitly to whatever server config or PROJ
list rack.maps maintains.

\~ 






An example of a command that generates a composite image of Pseudo CAPPI product of 12 radars:  

\~conf{.sh}
{
  "shell": true,
  "append_args": "--gAlign 'HORZ_FILL:VERT_FILL' -o composite-fi_DATA.png -o composite-fi_DATA.svg"
}
\~



\~exec{.sh}
rack \
    --cSize '640,768' \
    --cProj '3067' \
    --cBBox '-208000.0,6390000.0,1072000.0,7926000.0' \
    --cMethod 'WAVG,1,8' \
    --script "--pCappi 500 --cAdd" \
    data/*.h5 \
    --cExtract channels=DATA,WEIGHT \
    -o composite-fi.h5
\~


\~stop
\~

\~remark
removed include composite-fi_rack.sh
removed linkfile{composite-fi_rack.sh}
\~

\image html  composite-fi.svg.png  "A radar composite image – Finnish (FMI) radars, Pseudo CAPPI 600m, weighted-average compositing method: main product (left) and quality field (right)." 
\image latex composite-fi.svg.png  "A radar composite image – Finnish (FMI) radars, Pseudo CAPPI 600m, weighted-average compositing method: main product (left) and quality field (right)."   width=\textwidth


\b Experimental. The above command line can be generated – and optionally executed –
using Python utility \c rack.composite .
The equivalent command line is not remarkably shorter, but now the order of the options is insignificant:

\include composite-fi_py.sh

\~conf{.py}
{
  "workdir": "env/composite-fi/",
  "imports": ["logging", "rack.prog","rack.composite"],
  "prepend_code": ["logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)"],
  "append_code": [
	"# rack_cmd = prog.to_string(fmt)"
   ],
  "tests" : {
  	"rack.sh": {
  	  "returncode": 0
  	}
  }
}
\~ 

\note In above examples, the input files can be given equivalently using shell wildcards, with a single plain argument like: \c data/20*.fi*.h5 . That also makes option \c --inputPrefix or \c --INDIR (for Python) unneeded.

The above command lines can be generated also inside a Python program, as follows.

\~remark code{.py}
 
# Initialize command line generator.
composer = rack.cmdline.Composer(rack.composite)

# Set parameters
# To see thet supported command keywords, issue: python3 -m rack.composite

composer.set(PROJ=3067)
composer.set(BBOX=[-208000,6390000,1072000,7926000])
composer.set(SIZE=[640,768])
composer.set(METHOD='WAVG,1,8')

# composer.set(INDIR='data/') 
composer.set(INFILE='data/202609261800_radar.polar.fi???.h5') 

composer.set(PPROD='pCappi,500')
# Alternatively, a measured parameter selected as such: 
# composer.set(select='quantity=DBZH')

# composer.set(OUTDIR='out')
composer.set(OUTFILE='out/composite-fi.h5')

# Optional SVG image output
composer.set(FORMATS='svg')
composer.set(svgRelativePaths=True)
composer.set(map='out/map-composite-fi.png')
composer.set(svgStyle='.IMAGE_BORDER=stroke:gray;width:1px')


# Extract command sequence
prog = composer.get_prog()

# Show generated command line: 
fmt = rack.cmdline.RackFormatter(params_format="'{params}'") 
print(prog.to_string(fmt))

# ... or execute directly:
# rack.process.run(prog, logger=logger)
\~ endcode


\include{doc} cBBox-cSize-cMethod-select.hlp.md


See also
